Code statements:
```c
if (static_cpu_has_bug(X86_BUG_SYSRET_SS_ATTRS)) {
    unsigned short ss_sel;
    savesegment(ss, ss_sel);
    if (ss_sel != __KERNEL_DS)
        loadsegment(ss, __KERNEL_DS);
}
```

Code statements:
```c
if (static_cpu_has_bug(X86_BUG_SYSRET_SS_ATTRS)) {
    unsigned short ss_sel;
    savesegment(ss, ss_sel);
    if (ss_sel != __KERNEL_DS)
        loadsegment(ss, __KERNEL_DS);
}
```

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ss_sel != __KERNEL_DS)
    loadsegment(ss, __KERNEL_DS);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because AMD CPUs do not reinitialize the SS descriptor on executing the SYSRET instruction. If SYSRET is executed with SS set to NULL, it leads to an invalid user mode state where SS appears to be equal to `__USER_DS`, but actually causes a #SS fault when accessed. This situation arises during context switches or interrupts, where the SS may inadvertently be set to NULL.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
savesegment(ss, ss_sel);
if (ss_sel != __KERNEL_DS)
    loadsegment(ss, __KERNEL_DS);
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that the SS selector is set to `__KERNEL_DS` during every context switch. By checking if the current SS is not equal to `__KERNEL_DS` and loading it if necessary, the code prevents the scenario where SYSRET could be executed with SS set to NULL, thus avoiding the invalid user mode state and the resulting #SS fault. This workaround effectively guarantees that SS is always valid when transitioning back to user mode.

